package com.androidtutorials.myapplication

fun main() {
    demoLambda(
        "Calling Lambda",
        { a: Int, b: Int -> }
    )
}

fun demoLambda(text: String, operation: (Int, Int) -> Unit) {
    println("Hello World")
}

/*
------------------------------------------------------------
EXPLANATION
------------------------------------------------------------

1) While calling a lambda, we cannot pass direct values as parameters.

   ❌ Wrong:
       { 10, 20 -> }

   Because the left side of -> must always be VARIABLE NAMES,
   not actual values.

   ✅ Correct:
       { a: Int, b: Int -> }

   Here:
       a and b are parameter variables.
       Kotlin will assign values to them when the lambda is invoked.

------------------------------------------------------------

2) If the lambda has empty implementation:

   Since the return type is Unit,
   we just need to write the arrow (->) and we can leave the body empty.

   Example:
       { a: Int, b: Int -> }

   This is valid because:
       (Int, Int) -> Unit
       means it does not need to return anything.

------------------------------------------------------------

3) Lambda Structure Reminder:

   General Format:
       { parameters -> body }

   Even if body is empty, the arrow (->) is mandatory.

------------------------------------------------------------
*/

